[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports hold two problems, and
the queued fix reaches only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
A parser fix leaves the hint problem in place. Keep the booking-v3 form reports open until the
hint is fixed too.
</Callout>

## What the reports hold

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Each report counts once, by its id, because the export pages overlap. The cause comes from the
report's tenant and channel, as `causes.ts` runs it. Do not count intake's tag: it is a guess from
the reporter's browser, and the table shows how far it strays.

The table's last column reads the desk's export, which predates the two tickets below.

## The two problems

**The shared parser reads dates month first.** A tenant that writes the day first gets the day and
month swapped. This is what #4471 describes. Ticket:
[parser](https://github.com/jasondashaer/mega-dev-challenges/issues/4).

**The booking-v3 form prints the wrong hint.** Its date field says `MM/DD/YYYY`, so people type the
month first, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. These reports came through
that form from a day-first tenant. Ticket:
[hint](https://github.com/jasondashaer/mega-dev-challenges/issues/5).

Both look the same in a report: a date with its day and month swapped. So the body does not tell
them apart. The way the report came in and the form the tenant runs do.

## What is still unproven

The one-day reports in the note under the table are counted where they are filed. A swap of day and
month does not explain a one-day move, so check them again once the parser fix is live.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line. The two problems
share subject lines: every subject on a hint report also appears on parser reports. So the rule
could not keep them apart. The decision stays as
written; a new record has to replace it.

